The repair for plugin target identity — NOT YET APPLIED, and deliberately so.

WHAT IT DOES

  Two statements in the plugin branch of TestCaseComponent.record(): ask
  findExistingTarget for the target the plugin named, and only fall back to
  createOrResolveTarget when there is not one yet.

WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT

  The change belongs in INGenious/, which is a submodule this lane was told to
  read and build but not to modify or re-pin — three lanes are working against
  that base and its pin move is a decision with a re-proof list attached
  (https://github.com/Wladefant/ing-qa-automation/issues/161). So the repair was
  written, compiled and behaviour-proved, and is handed over as a patch rather
  than smuggled into a shared dependency.

  It belongs in the SAME commit as the pin move. Do not apply it, move the pin
  later, and let a green build sit in between: that green build is the misleading
  one. (Applying it before the pin move is harmless — see "at our pin" below —
  but it proves nothing on its own.)

WHY IT IS NEEDED

  Upstream https://github.com/ing-bank/INGenious/pull/302 fixes a real bug — a
  person who types the same test-case name into the recording-target dialog twice
  used to overwrite their own first recording — by making createOrResolveTarget
  never reuse a test case. It now always creates a uniquely suffixed one.

  The method kept its name, its signature and its callers. Only its meaning
  changed. The plugin path routes the plugin-chosen target through that exact
  method, and its whole point is stable identity: a plugin says "this belongs to
  test case X", and re-recording X has to land in X. After #302 the same call
  produces X, X_1, X_2 — with a clean merge, a compiling build and no warning.

  findExistingTarget already exists at upstream head, and #302 left it with zero
  callers. The repair picks it back up. It leaves upstream's dialog fix entirely
  intact: the dialog does not go through findExistingTarget and keeps uniquifying.

HOW TO APPLY

  cd INGenious
  git apply ../ingenious-plugin/harness/repair/plugin-target-identity.patch

  Written against our fork commit e2aa28a4 (feat/studio-panel-plugins), and
  checked with `git apply --check`:

    our pin e2aa28a4                                  applies
    the three-way merge of e2aa28a4 with 7bc763b6     applies   <- the pin move
    upstream head 7bc763b6 on its own                 DOES NOT

  The last line is not a defect and is written down so nobody reads a failure
  there as a stale patch: upstream has no plugin recording-target branch at all,
  so there is nothing for these context lines to attach to. The file the pin move
  actually produces is the middle one, and that is the one that matters.

  Applied to that merged file, the `source` square of the guard flips from red to
  green and names the reason: limb A false, limb B true — identity no longer
  depends on upstream keeping createOrResolveTarget's old meaning.

WHAT WAS PROVED, AND WHERE

  * It compiles at our current pin. A temp tree from `git archive HEAD` of the
    submodule (never the shared target/, which has produced a stale-binary false
    verdict three times) was built twice with JDK 17:
        mvn -pl IDE -am -DskipTests -Dprettier.skip=true compile
    control (unpatched) exit 0, patched exit 0.
  * The behaviour, both directions:
        bash ingenious-plugin/harness/run-plugin-target-identity-harness.sh
    Seven squares, one of which must be RED. `after-302` — upstream's resolution
    step with today's plugin branch — fails, and prints the fork:
        the plugin asked for : 3951650 - Partner-Suche + Kunde-360
        and the second recording went to : 3951650 - Partner-Suche + Kunde-360_1
    `after-302-repaired` — the same core with the two statements below — passes.
  * At our pin the repair is behaviourally inert: createOrResolveTarget still
    resolves, so findExistingTarget finds what it would have returned. The
    `repaired` square proves that, and that nothing that used to be created has
    stopped being created — new target, new scenario, reusable half kept separate
    from the ordinary one of the same name.

WHAT WAS NOT PROVED

  Nothing was run in a live Studio. The recorder only reaches this code with
  Playwright and a browser up; the doubles reproduce the resolution step, not the
  recorder. After the pin moves, re-record a plugin-named target twice in a real
  Studio and confirm one test case — that is item 1 of the re-proof list in
  https://github.com/Wladefant/ing-qa-automation/blob/main/docs/reference/UPSTREAM-DRIFT-3.1.0-2026-07-28.md

